+    # Actually, the requirement says: "stops returning a registry once the application has shut down."
+    # This is usually checked by seeing if the lifespan context manager has exited.
+    
+    # In many cases, the registry object itself might stay, but we want to 
+    # ensure it's the "running" one.
+    
+    # One way to check "running" is to see if the state was actually populated.
+    # But let's look at how other integrations do it.
+    # They don't have a "shutdown" check, they just check if it exists.
+    # But our requirement 5 says we must.
+    
+    # Let's check if the lifespan instance's registry is still valid.
+    # The registry object itself doesn't have a "closed" state easily visible 
